            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "cooley3211"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I always put my potatoes back into the empty pan to help get rid of excess water. Also, I never use a mixer to mash my potatoes because overmixing makes them gluey. Better to invest in a potato ricer or food mill for mashing. Finally, just crush the garlic and put it in the pan with the butter to melt. When the garlic begins to brown, remove it and add your potatoes and milk/cream to the pot. Get the garlic flavor without having to bite into a piece of garlic. Season to taste.",
            "datePublished": "2023-12-01T17:53:19.890Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "kathyisnyder"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used red potatoes and added the garlic cloves (whole) the last 10 minutes of boiling. This came out very good, except I did need to add an extra tablespoon of the milk to make it a bit creamier. I also added garlic salt to give it a bit more flavor. I first mashed the potatoes with a hand masher, then used an electric mixer to finish it to the right texture.",
            "datePublished": "2018-11-22T18:00:52.973Z"
          },
